# jsonTools local CI configuration.
#
# Copy to .ci.env (which is gitignored) and edit what you need. This file is
# documentation: every knob below has a default in tools/ci.sh, so with no .ci.env
# at all the repo behaves exactly as described here.
#
#   cp .ci.env.example .ci.env

# Where the JSOM source lives. jsonTools links JSOM in as a subdirectory, and the
# default is to FetchContent it from GitHub — which needs the network and floats on
# JSOM's main branch, so a build can change under you without jsonTools changing at
# all. Pointing at a checkout you already have makes the gate offline, fast, and
# attributable: every run prints the JSOM revision it built against, so a break
# coming from JSOM is visible instead of mysterious.
#
# Default: the sibling checkout (../JSOM) when it exists, else FetchContent.
# CI_JSOM_DIR=/home/harri/hermes-workspace/JSOM

# Parallel jobs for the build/asan/pristine stages.
CI_JOBS=4

# Stages run when no stage names are given on the command line.
CI_DEFAULT_STAGES="tree format kitprobes build tests cli asan fuzz tidy wire version pristine"

# 1 = a missing tool (clang-format, clang-tidy) FAILS the run instead of SKIPping it.
# Set to 1 on a machine that is supposed to have them, so a silently missing gate
# cannot pass unnoticed — a gate that prints "not found" and exits 0 is not a gate.
CI_STRICT_TOOLS=0

# 1 = keep the pristine-checkout temp directory after a run (path is printed).
CI_KEEP_TMP=0

# Where per-stage output goes. Gitignored; on failure the tail is printed and the
# path is named.
CI_LOG_DIR=.ci-logs

# clang-tidy baseline — OPTIONAL. With no file, the tidy stage requires ZERO findings.
# Create one only to tolerate findings you inherited, and create it with the gate:
#
#   tools/ci.sh --write-tidy-baseline
#   git add .ci/tidy-baseline.txt && git commit -m "ci: accept the inherited clang-tidy findings"
#
# That one command runs the build stage (tidy refuses to run without
# $CI_BUILD_DIR/compile_commands.json — the build stage is what writes it), runs tidy, and
# writes every finding it reports into this file, then prints the `git add` line: the
# baseline is part of the repo, and the tree stage fails on a file that is neither
# committed nor ignored. Its tidy stage failing during that capture is expected — with no
# baseline yet, every inherited finding counts as new.
#
# Do NOT capture the file by hand from .ci-logs/tidy.log. The comparison normalises BOTH
# sides (tidy_key in tools/ci.sh): the repo root is stripped from every path, and
# `:line:column` is stripped, so one finding is still one finding after code moves above it,
# and a baseline captured in one clone still describes a checkout at another path (the
# nightly clean checkout, a colleague's machine). A raw copy of the log matches none of
# that, so every baselined finding reads as new and the tidy stage is permanently red — it
# cannot pass at all, on any machine. There is exactly one definition of the form, and it
# lives in the script; ask the script for the file.
#
# One honest consequence of the line-blind comparison: a SECOND identical finding in a file
# that already has one collapses into the first. Fix the baselined finding rather than
# growing the baseline around it.
CI_TIDY_BASELINE=.ci/tidy-baseline.txt

# Build directories. The sanitizer one is separate on purpose so sanitizer objects
# never mix with the normal build; the fuzz one for the same reason, plus clang++.
CI_BUILD_DIR=build
CI_ASAN_BUILD_DIR=build-asan
CI_FUZZ_BUILD_DIR=build-fuzz

# Seconds of libFuzzer smoke in the fuzz stage. This is the gate's SHARE of the
# fuzzing: the harness is built and run here so a pushed commit cannot land a
# crash the pointer traversal already knows how to find, while the long campaign
# belongs to the nightly cron (fuzz_overnight.sh). Raising it slows every push by
# exactly that much for coverage the cron is already paid to cover; lowering it
# below ~10 s makes the stage mostly startup.
CI_FUZZ_SECONDS=60
